File Index

/account-store.test.ts
Purpose: createAccount/duplicate rejection, authenticate, changePassword/adminSetTemporaryPassword epoch bumps, email verify + password reset flows, globalRole set/clear + unknown-tier 400, corrupt-file lockdown.

/account-store.ts
Purpose: Username accounts — scrypt password hashing, the epoch counter both change-password and rotate-master bump, pending email-verify/password-reset token flows; hashes BEFORE re-checking for a duplicate username (closes a TOCTOU race scrypt's latency would otherwise leave open). Phase A: optional globalRole tier ('user' = field absent) via setGlobalRole (host scripts + A2's strictly-below admin routes); A2 adds listAll (full records — routes must map to public fields).

/app.ts
Purpose: Registers all HTTP routes on an Express app (health check + the eight route modules incl. Phase A's sso + token-exchange, A2's admin, and device); sets trust proxy for the per-IP challenge throttle behind nginx.

/audit-log.test.ts
Purpose: JSONL shape — one line per entry, timestamp, action/detail round-trip.

/audit-log.ts
Purpose: A2 append-only JSONL audit trail (role changes, temp-passwords) under DATA_DIR — written for the operator's grep, never read back by the app; mirrors mud-builder's audit.log precedent.

/config.ts
Purpose: Typed env accessor (PORT, DATA_DIR, DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEY/_FILE, SMTP_*, PUBLIC_ORIGIN, DEVICE_ORIGIN_SERVICES, DEVICE_GRANT_REQUIRED_SERVICES). DEVICE_ORIGIN_SERVICES parses `origin=svc|svc,origin=svc` into a Map and is the AUTHORITY on a device's audience — it mirrors nginx's origin -> upstream routing, so an origin may reach exactly the upstream the edge routes it to. deviceAllowedOrigins is DERIVED from its keys rather than configured separately, so an origin permitted to enroll always has a defined audience and the CORS allowlist cannot drift from the audience map. Trailing slashes trimmed (a browser Origin never has one); malformed entries are collected as deviceConfigWarnings and SKIPPED rather than thrown — a typo in an optional feature must not take down login for everyone, and skipping fails closed. Empty = deny all, deliberately.

/crypto-primitives.test.ts
Purpose: Encrypt/decrypt round-trip + tamper rejection, loadDataKey's full precedence chain including first-boot self-generation and reuse, sign/verify round-trip + wrong-keypair rejection.

/crypto-primitives.ts
Purpose: AES-256-GCM at-rest envelope (encryptJson/decryptJson, throws on a bad tag), loadDataKey()'s env-var/mounted-file/self-generate-on-first-boot precedence, and Ed25519 helpers (generateServiceKeypair/signAssertion/verifyAssertion) for the service-to-service introspect guard.

/deps.ts
Purpose: AuthServerDeps — the stores/mailer/throttle/loginLockout/publicOrigin/device-policy bundle threaded through registerRoutes (deviceOriginServices, deviceGrantRequiredServices, and a DeviceRateLimiters pair keyed per-IP and per-DEVICE — the latter is the limit nginx cannot express, since a zone can only key on the address); kept separate from app.ts to avoid a type-only import cycle with the route modules.

/device-store.ts
Purpose: Registry of enrolled browser devices for the device-bound credential scheme — one record per browser holding the PUBLIC half of a non-extractable ECDSA P-256 keypair whose private half never leaves that browser's IndexedDB. `lookup(deviceId, currentEpoch)` is the single gate every device authentication passes, returning a REASON ('unknown'|'revoked'|'stale-epoch'|'stale-binding') not just null, because the client must tell "re-enroll me" from "gone for good" and an operator must tell a benign password change from a deliberate revoke. Each record stores enrolledAtEpoch and lookup compares it to the account's CURRENT epoch — copied from key-store's mintedAtEpoch idiom so password change / reset / temp-password invalidate enrollments AUTOMATICALLY, with no list of call sites anyone has to remember (revokeAllForAccount is a separate user-facing panic button, NOT the epoch mechanism). Each record ALSO freezes enrolledOrigin + allowedServices, resolved from config's origin map at enrollment: this is what makes a token's audience meaningful, because the service named in an assert request is only ever checked AGAINST that frozen list (static mayMintFor) and never trusted on its own — otherwise an XSS on any enrolled app could mint a token for a more privileged one. Frozen at enrollment rather than re-resolved per assert so re-pointing the origin map cannot silently widen a device already in the field. A record with no allowedServices (written before the binding existed) is 'stale-binding' and must re-enroll — inferring an audience would be inventing an authorization nobody granted. NOTHING here is a secret (a public key and a deviceId are both safe to leak), which is the point: no at-rest credential remains to steal on either side. Separate file (auth-devices.json) from auth-keys.json for the same lock-contention reason key-store split from account-store — these records are near-static while device TOKENS churn every few minutes. assertValidP256PublicJwk REFUSES a jwk containing 'd' rather than stripping it: an exported private key means the client broke non-extractability, which is a client bug to surface, not to paper over. Revocation is a tombstone, never a delete, so revoked devices stay auditable; revoke() is account-scoped so one account can never revoke another's. revokeAllForAccount is called on any epoch bump — the epoch already kills outstanding TOKENS via key-store.verify, but enrollments must die too, or a password changed BECAUSE it leaked would leave an attacker-enrolled device minting fresh tokens indefinitely.

/device-store.test.ts
Purpose: DeviceStore + assertValidP256PublicJwk coverage. Public JWKs under test are GENERATED via node crypto (never hand-written literals) so the accepted shape cannot drift from what WebCrypto actually emits. Pins the security-relevant refusals: a private jwk carrying 'd', wrong kty/curve, missing/empty coordinates, cross-account revoke, and that listForAccount never leaks publicKeyJwk (asserted by exact key-set equality, so a future field addition fails loudly rather than silently exposing material).

/device-nonce-store.ts
Purpose: Single-use, short-lived (2min) challenge nonces for device assertion — in memory only, same "no new dependency, restart clears" tradeoff as LoginLockout/ChallengeThrottle (losing nonces on restart is invisible: the client just asks for another). A server-issued nonce is used INSTEAD of a client timestamp deliberately — a timestamp window must tolerate clock skew, which keeps a captured signature valid for that whole window, whereas a nonce dies the instant it is spent and there is no clock to disagree about. consume() deletes on ANY found entry, so a failed attempt (wrong device, wrong signature) still burns it and it can never be ground against. Sweeps expired entries on issue(), the only write path, so abandoned challenges (every closed tab) cannot accumulate.

/device-nonce-store.test.ts
Purpose: Injectable-clock coverage (no real sleeps) — single use, per-device binding, burn-on-failed-attempt, TTL boundary both sides, expiresAt consistency, and that issue() sweeps stale entries while keeping live ones.

/encrypted-file-store.ts
Purpose: Generic base shared by account/key/service-key stores — atomic tmp+rename writes of an encrypted envelope, mtime-cached reload, corrupt/undecryptable file LOCKS the store rather than silently resetting it.

/global-tiers.ts
Purpose: The AUTHORITATIVE hub-global tier ladder (owner > admin > moderator > user) + isGlobalTier guard; services-server's auth-tiers.ts mirrors it for consumers (deliberate duplication across the app boundary, same rationale as the assertion format).

/errors.ts
Purpose: AuthError — the one typed failure-with-status class shared across every store and route in this app (not cross-imported from mud-builder-server, but centralized once here rather than duplicated per store). 2026-07-30 gained an optional third `code` arg, emitted by safe() alongside the message, for failures a client must BRANCH on rather than display (DEVICE_REENROLL_REQUIRED → start enrollment). Generalizes the hand-rolled `code: 'MUST_CHANGE_PASSWORD'` session-guard.ts already returned directly. Never put anything in a code that the message wouldn't already reveal.

/index.ts
Purpose: HTTP listener — env loading, fail-fast on an unusable data encryption key (never starts serving without one), builds the AuthServerDeps bundle, listens.

/key-store.test.ts
Purpose: Mint/verify for all kinds, expiresAt null-vs-dated-vs-expired, epoch-mismatch invalidation, listKeys shape (excludes exchange tokens), rotate/revoke ownership 404s, revokeById, Phase A exchange-token audience/TTL/purge behavior. 2026-07-30 added device-token coverage: kind/audience/short-TTL shape, one token PER audience (neither carries the other's service), expiry + epoch-bump invalidation, absence from listKeys, and both purge edges — a record 31min past expiry is dropped while one that expired seconds ago is KEPT (the 30min debug grace), with api/sso records proven untouched by the device purge.

/key-store.ts
Purpose: API keys, browser sessions, AND Phase A exchange tokens ('sso' 7d login / 'obo' 2min on-behalf-of, minted by mintExchangeToken with service = the token's AUDIENCE) as ONE record type — verify (sha256 + timingSafeEqual + not-revoked + not-expired + mintedAtEpoch === the account's CURRENT epoch, supplied by the caller so this store never reaches into account-store directly); revokeById for logout (no ownership/kind filter) vs. ownership-checked rotateKey/revokeKey for the key-management routes. mintExchangeToken purges sso/obo records >24h past expiry (they always expire, so they'd accumulate forever); countKind gives purge visibility, countForAccount gives A2's LIVE per-account credential counts (its Record<KeyKind, number> initialiser hardcodes every kind, so ADDING a KeyKind is a compile break there — by design, it forces you to decide). 2026-07-30: added kind 'device' + mintDeviceToken(accountId, service, currentEpoch, ttl=10min default) for the device-bound credential scheme — `service` is the AUDIENCE (Phase A service-isolation, so a browser mints one token per service rather than one valid everywhere), and it purges device records >30min past expiry. That purge is load-bearing, not tidiness: a 10-min token re-minted per open tab is ~144 records/day/tab and persist() rewrites+re-encrypts the WHOLE file per mint, so skipping it makes the store grow without bound and every mint slower. Device tokens flow through the unchanged verify() and therefore through /api/introspect, which is what lets downstream services adopt this with no code change.

/logger.ts
Purpose: Minimal winston console+JSON logger (deliberately not services-server's Logger — no file-rotation dependency needed here).

/login-lockout.test.ts
Purpose: Free-attempts-then-lock, natural expiry, escalating doubling-with-cap across repeat offenses, independent username/IP tracking (either locked blocks), recordSuccess resets, case/whitespace-insensitive username matching — via an injectable clock, no real sleeps.

/login-lockout.ts
Purpose: Phase 15 fail2ban-style escalating soft lockout for POST /api/auth/login — in-memory Map keyed by normalized username AND separately by source IP (either locked blocks the attempt); first N failures free, each further one doubles the lock duration up to a cap, recordSuccess clears both keys. No OS-level enforcement (plan constraint) — pure in-app counting, same "no new dependency" tradeoff as ChallengeThrottle.

/mailer.ts
Purpose: Pluggable mail transport — console/log fallback with zero SMTP config (must work for local dev), nodemailer transport when SMTP_HOST is set; used only by the two recovery-link flows (email verify, password reset).

/questions-store.test.ts
Purpose: Pool-too-small rejection, normalized case-insensitive answer matching, single-use + expiry enforcement, per-IP throttle capacity/refill/independence.

/questions-store.ts
Purpose: Anti-bot signup challenge — PLAIN (deliberately unencrypted, hand-edited) dsl-questions.json pool, mtime-reloaded; in-memory single-use challenge instances (3 random questions, 10min TTL, marks used even on failure); ChallengeThrottle is a basic per-IP token bucket, no new dependency.

/service-key-store.test.ts
Purpose: Valid assertion accept, unregistered/impostor-key rejection, two-key rotation window, revoked-key rejection, stale/future/over-wide-window rejection, nonce replay rejection, Phase A redirect-URI exact-match/validation/removal + isRegisteredService needing a live key.

/service-key-store.ts
Purpose: Per-service Ed25519 public-key registry for /api/introspect and /api/token-exchange — multiple non-revoked keys per service expected during a rotation window; verifyAssertion() peek-decodes the claimed service WITHOUT trusting it (used only to pick which keys to try), re-validates iat/exp/nonce off the cryptographically VERIFIED payload, and tracks seen nonces to reject replays. Phase A: per-service SSO redirectUris (exact-match, http(s) only, no fragments, add requires the service to pre-exist) + isRegisteredService (at least one non-revoked key); A2 adds listServices for the delegation surface.

/sso-code-store.test.ts
Purpose: Single-use redeem, burn-on-mismatched-service, exact redirect-URI match, 60s expiry via the injectable clock, unknown-code rejection.

/sso-code-store.ts
Purpose: One-time SSO authorization codes — in-memory ONLY by design (restart voids them), 60s TTL, single-use, bound to {account, service, redirectUri}; any redeem attempt BURNS the code even on a mismatch (no brute-forcing bindings against a stolen code), null on every failure without saying which check failed.
/rate-limit.ts
Purpose: In-process leaky-bucket limiter using nginx's OWN limit_req units (ratePerMinute + burst), so an in-service limit can be read against the edge zone it backs up. nginx stays PRIMARY — it sheds load before node is reached — so each call site sets a WIDER burst than its matching zone, meaning the edge always trips first and this only fires for traffic that bypassed it (direct-to-container, port-forward, misconfigured edge) or for keys nginx cannot see: $binary_remote_addr is the only thing a zone can key on, so per-DEVICE and per-ACCOUNT limits are only expressible here. Tokens are FRACTIONAL on purpose — whole-token refill would quantise 120r/m into one-per-500ms steps and refuse a correctly-paced caller. Rejection still records the refill, or a rejected bucket's clock freezes and that key is refused forever. sweep() recomputes each bucket's CURRENT level before deciding it is idle: reading the stored count is stale (a bucket only refreshes when its own key is seen again), so a flood key touched exactly once would never be swept — defeating the memory bound in precisely the scenario it exists for. Distinct from questions-store's ChallengeThrottle, which refills in whole 2-minute intervals for a human-paced flow; kept separate so neither compromises.

/rate-limit.test.ts
Purpose: RateLimiter coverage on an injectable clock (no real sleeps). Pins burst-then-refuse, sustained refill, fractional accumulation across two short waits, the burst ceiling after long idle, recovery after rejection (the latch bug), key independence, and that a sweep never RELEASES a currently-throttled key — otherwise a flood of new keys would be a way to clear an existing throttle. Note the 'keeps active buckets' property is asserted via that release check rather than by inspecting the map: a bucket distinguishable from absent is one with <burst tokens, while being allowed needs >=1 token, so at burst=1 the two coincide and survival is unobservable through the public API.

/config.test.ts
Purpose: parseOriginServices + getAuthServerConfig's device settings. Security-relevant config, so the failure modes are pinned individually: trailing-slash trimming (a browser Origin never has one), whitespace tolerance, multi-service via '|', duplicate origins UNIONing rather than one silently winning, and malformed entries warning-and-skipping while good entries survive. Also pins that deviceAllowedOrigins is DERIVED from the map keys — the invariant that stops the CORS allowlist drifting from the audience map.
